693 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(To reduce weight while maintaining rigidity, we used pocketed carbon fiber plates instead of traditional aluminum sideplates. Carbon fiber provides a higher strength-to-weight ratio, allowing the drivetrain to remain rigid during impacts, defense, and rapid acceleration.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

694 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(Through testing and comparison with aluminum, carbon fiber showed higher stiffness while significantly reducing weight, improving drivetrain stability, efficiency, and overall robot performance during high-speed movement.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

695 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(Using computational analysis tools and graph modeling, we created stress vs deformation curves to visualize how each material behaves under increasing load.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

696 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(Key results from our graph analysis:)/Pg 15 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

697 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(\200 Carbon fiber maintained lower deformation at higher stress levels)/Pg 15 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

698 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(\200 Aluminum reached structural flex earlier under identical loading conditions)/Pg 15 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

699 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(\200 Reduced deformation improves drivetrain alignment and gear efficiency)/Pg 15 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

700 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 407 0 R/E(These results reinforced our design choice to use pocketed carbon fiber plates for our drivetrain superstructure.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

